WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.389
This month, Congress will decide the fate
of the Export-Import Bank. EXIM is a

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:15.670
federal agency that subsidizes US
exports by extending taxpayer-backed

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:20.289
financing to overseas buyers, including
foreign governments and companies. Ex-Im's

00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:24.340
charter expires September 30th, but
Congress has taken up a bill to renew

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.050
the agency for ten more years. Without
EXIMs loans and loan guarantees, the

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.340
sponsors argue, US exporters would suffer
from a lack of financing. But that's not

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.809
what recent history has shown us. In 2015,
conservative opposition caused the

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.180
bank's charter to lapse and its board to
lose a quorum, so for nearly four years

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.079
EXIM couldn't finance large deals. What
happened to financing for aircraft, which

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:51.010
is EXIM's main line of business? It
blossomed like never before!

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:56.170
You see, Boeing assembled a consortium of
lenders and insurers who found all sorts

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.370
of innovative ways to finance planes.
That's not surprising: When government

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.199
steps out of the way, the market steps in.
EXIM's clients do like government

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:09.880
financing, but that doesn't mean they
need it. What do you think? Should

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.009
Congress reauthorize Export-Import Bank
or kill it? Let us know in our poll. Also,

00:01:15.009 --> 00:01:19.090
let us know what other topics you'd like
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:25.200
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

